==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pro-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|processioun}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|pourciession}}, from {{der|en|la|prōcessiō||a marching forward, an advance, in Late Latin a religious procession}}, from {{m|la|prōcēdere}}, past participle {{m|la|prōcessus||to move forward, advance, proceed}}; see {{m|en|proceed}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɹəˈsɛʃən/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|pro|ces|sion}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-procession.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The act of [[progress]]ing or [[proceed]]ing.
#* {{RQ:Pearson Exposition of the Creed}}
#*:From whence it came to pass in the primitive times , that the Latin fathers taught expressly the '''procession''' of the Spirit from the Father and the Son
#* {{RQ:Trench Justin Martyr|poem=The Same Continued|page=126|passage=Yet proof is here of men's unquenched desire / That the '''procession''' of their life might be / More equable majestic pure and free; {{...}}}}
# A [[group]] of people or things moving along in an [[orderly]], stately, or solemn manner; a train of persons advancing in order; a [[retinue]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''procession''' of mourners}}
#: {{ux|en|the Lord Mayor's '''procession'''}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|act=II|scene=i|page=126|column=2|passage=Here comes the towneſ-men, on '''Proceſſion''', / To preſent your Highneſſe with the man.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=I|page=7|passage=By reason of these things, then, the whaling voyage was welcome; the great flood-gates of the wonder-world swung open, and in the wild conceits that swayed me to my purpose, two and two there floated into my inmost soul, endless '''processions''' of the whale, and, mid most of them all, one grand hooded phantom, like a snow hill in the air.}}
#* '''1914''', ''Westways'' (volume 6, page 7)
#*: {{quote|en|The final fifty miles of the race was a '''procession''' with little change in the relative positions of the cars {{...}}}}
# A number of things [[happening]] in [[sequence]] (in [[space]] or in [[time]]).
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical|obsolete|in the plural}} [[litany|Litanies]] said in procession and not kneeling.
#*{{quote-book|en|1894|{{w|Orby Shipley}}|Carmina Mariana|passage=In many a form I see thee oft <br>In myriad manners are thy praises told <br>In old '''processions''' carved on Grecian urns}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} The [[rapid]] [[dismissal]] of a [[series]] of [[batsman|batsmen]].
#* {{quote-book|en|2012|K. L. Mohana Varma|Cricket-Indo: The Story of an Indo-Pak One-Day Cricket Turf War|page=205|text=Before he closed and opened his eyes, the bails on the wicket behind Johnny Masih were shattered. That was the beginning of a '''procession'''. The second ball clean bowled the batsman. The third ball was a catch for the wicketkeeper.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|2015|Steve Dolman|Edwin Smith: A Life in Derbyshire Cricket|page=36|text=Scotland moved nicely to 45 without loss before I took the first wicket and then it became a '''procession'''.}}

